 one_liner="Adding a disutility-of-schooling term to a structural education-choice model is equivalent to assuming a relationship between wealth, risk and schooling: with the term present, the utility return to education falls with wealth and, when agents have a precautionary saving motive, rises with the riskiness of future consumption.",
 abstract="Studies on structural education choice models are often inconsistent in choosing whether and how to include a disutility of education, especially in an environment with risk and wealth inequality. We show that adding a disutility term to the education decision, a human capital investment option, is equivalent to assuming a relationship between wealth, risk, and education. Utility gain from education is increasing in the riskiness of future consumption. A riskier environment further propels an agent to choose the human capital investment option that maximizes future income. If the degree of risk increases heterogeneously across multiple human capital investment options, risk aversion and the precautionary savings motive can compound or negate each other depending on which option has a greater increase in risk.",
 findings=[
  "Proposition 1: if schooling has a positive financial return and marginal utility is convex (precautionary saving), a mean-preserving spread in future consumption risk raises the utility return to education.",
  "Proposition 2: if both schooling and working become riskier but schooling’s risk rises by not too much more, the precautionary-saving effect still dominates risk aversion and schooling becomes more attractive.",
  "The utility return to education is decreasing in initial wealth (extending Belley and Lochner 2007 to a risky environment), so the range of human capital for which an agent chooses school narrows with wealth.",
  "Implication for estimation: counterfactuals from models that include a disutility term embed these wealth–risk–schooling relations by assumption; models that omit it may understate the role of wealth and risk.",
 ],
